  "summary": "A distinctive South African architectural style blending Dutch, German, French and Indonesian influences.",
  "description": "Features elegant gables, whitewashed walls, thatched roofs, and symmetrical layouts, developed by Dutch settlers and adapted to local conditions.",
  "culturalContext": "Cape Dutch architecture (late 1600s-early 1800s) grew from VOC colonial farms in the Cape: whitewashed walls of local lime-and-shell plaster, thatch roofs, and above all the front gable — from simple holbol round curves to elaborate neoclassical bell gables — that marked the wine estate's face. Groot Constantia and Boschendal preserve the canon; the style became South Africa's white-heritage emblem and still shapes Cape Winelands tourism design.",
